# REGRESSION — seeding an `on error:` handler must contribute its USES
# only, never its KILLS. Walking the handler into the caller's live set
# deletes a name the normal path still reads: ICE rc 101 on both lanes.
#
# The liveness walker is UNDER-APPROXIMATION-CRITICAL: a use it cannot
# see makes a variable look dead at an earlier consuming position, so
# the value is MOVED instead of cloned and the later read finds freed
# memory. RED-verified against the pre-fix compiler.
#
# The `v.set(0, ...)` is load-bearing: it frees the value moved into
# the vector. Without it the moved-from read still finds live memory.
# PROBE K1 -- the SEED applies KILLS, not just uses. `seed_on_error_uses`
# (liveness.rs:82-90) calls `walk_block` on the handler body with the CALLER's
# live set, and `walk_block` applies kills as well as uses. At the top level
# that set starts EMPTY, so the kill is harmless; inside a nested block it is
# the enclosing block's live-out, so a kill inside a handler registered there
# deletes a name live on the NORMAL path -- the exact leak `606a7c4e`
# fixed for
# the un-nested shape.
#
# `unsafe:` is the wrapper because its arm passes `live` straight through
# (liveness.rs:236-238); `if` unions with `saved` and masks it.
#
# The `v.set(0, ...)` is load-bearing: it frees the value moved into the
# vector.
String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

int f() throws String:
    String s = mk("abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789", "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ")
    Vector[String] v = []
    v.push(s)
    v.set(0, mk("xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx", "yyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy"))
    unsafe:
        on error:
            s = mk("c", "d")
    print(s)
    return 1

void main():
    int r = f()! catch (e): -1
    print(r)
